# Shell scripts must keep Unix line endings even when checked out on Windows.
# Git for Windows rewrites them to CRLF by default, which turns a script's
# "#!/bin/sh" into "#!/bin/sh\r" — and Linux then reports the script itself
# as "not found", because it is looking for an interpreter whose name ends
# in a carriage return. The container is built from a Windows checkout, so
# this is not hypothetical.
*.sh text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
docker-compose.yml text eol=lf

# The other way round: Windows batch files need CRLF, and a .cmd checked out
# with Unix endings can fail in ways as obscure as the shebang problem above.
# PowerShell is happy either way; pinned for consistency with its launcher.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
